WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.896
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.820
SPEAKER: Google Cloud recently
announced the new PostgreSQL

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.650
interface for Cloud Spanner.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:17.380
This combines the power
and reliability of Spanner

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.990
with the familiarity
of Postgres.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.570
Postgres is a well-established,
open-source database

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.580
with a vibrant and
growing ecosystem

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:26.620
that many development
and ops teams are already

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:27.910
familiar with.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:30.910
The Postgres interface
allows organizations

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.280
to take advantage of
Google's managed relational

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.030
database using standard tools
and skills from the Postgres

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:38.740
ecosystem.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.860
The interface supports Spanner's
rich feature set, as well as

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.940
common Postgres data
types and SQL syntax.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.830
In this video, I will
show you how easy

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:50.860
it is to set up a Spanner
database with the PostgreSQL

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:55.660
interface, then integrate that
into a Python application using

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.210
the open source
Spanner client library.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.910
And finally, I'll use the
standard Postgres psql

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:04.000
command-line tool to
explore the database.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.670
First, let's create a
new Spanner instance

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:09.430
using the Cloud Console.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:12.400
I'm creating a small instance
for this test with Spanner's

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.110
granular instance sizing.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:17.080
I can easily scale that up
later as needed without downtime

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.540
or rearchitecture.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.390
A Spanner instance can
host multiple databases.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:25.070
If you already have an
existing Spanner instance,

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:27.590
you will need to create a new
database with the Postgres

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.710
dialect support.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.840
When I create a
database, I can choose

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.430
between Google Standard SQL
and PostgreSQL as the dialect.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.800
I'll select the
PostgreSQL option.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.010
Both dialects support the
same set of capabilities.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:45.460
It's important to mention that
the Postgres interface does not

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:48.670
implement all of
PostgreSQL functionality.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.730
Rather, it provides Postgres
syntax for existing Spanner

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.640
features to avoid having
to learn new semantics when

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.640
you begin using Spanner.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:01.570
So next I will
create a users table.

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:04.570
I'll use Postgres
DDL and data types.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:07.270
Because it's just Postgres,
this schema change

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:11.330
would run the same in any
other Postgres environment.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.140
So this makes applications
written against the Postgre

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:17.110
interface more portable.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.720
It's worth mentioning that you
can use the gcloud command line

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.480
tool to create the
database if you prefer.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.830
Just make sure to specify the
--database-dialect option.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:29.230
Leveraging one of the
supported Spanner drivers,

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:30.970
you can start
writing applications

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.090
against the database we
just created right away.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:35.800
Besides the Spanner
client library,

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.460
no additional
software is needed.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:43.150
For example, we have a
Python application here

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:46.360
that connects to our database
and leverages the PostgreSQL

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:47.560
interface.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.290
I've downloaded the Spanner
Python client library

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:54.550
and installed it via pip
into my virtual environment.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:57.130
In this simple application,
I import the library

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:00.260
and define the
instance and database.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.740
Then I issue an insert
into our users table

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:04.960
using familiar Postgres syntax.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.050
For instance, I'm not having to
specify a column list, like I

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:09.940
would with Spanner's dialect.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:13.300
You'll also notice that I use
the Postgres NOW() function

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.190
to populate the
timestamp columns,

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:17.290
rather than the
CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() function

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:19.990
available in Spanner's
default SQL dialect.

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:27.460
I just showed how we could
use the native Spanner

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:32.120
driver to run Postgres syntax
SQL from a Python application.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.320
But what about other tools, like
psql, that don't know anything

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:36.880
about Spanner?

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:40.180
For those, we provided
a proxy called PGAdapter

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.420
that translates the Postgres
wire protocol into Spanner's

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:45.850
own gRPC-based protocol.

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.040
To set it up, you will
need to authenticate

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.530
with the gloud command-line
tool to generate

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.810
the credentials required
to connect to your Spanner

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:53.800
instance.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:57.310
Then download the
pgadapter.jar file and run it.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:59.620
That's it.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:02.230
With PGAdapter running,
the only thing left

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.270
is to connect with psql.

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:08.140
I can use psql syntax
to list out the tables.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:12.250
Now I can create a unique index
on the email field in our Users

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:15.730
table, again leveraging
Postgres syntax.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.209
Next, I want to query
the information schema.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:21.130
You can see that the
Postgres data types

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:24.310
are displayed for the columns.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.070
Initially, PGAdapter is only
provided for development

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:29.440
scenarios with
psql, but we'd like

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:33.620
to support a broader range
of Postgres tools eventually.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:35.770
So that's how easy it
is to set up a Spanner

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.990
instance with the new
PostgreSQL interface

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:41.770
and get started using it in
your application and with psql.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:44.800
If you want Google's highly
available and scalable managed

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.580
relational database and already
know Postgres' SQL syntax,

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:50.470
this is for you.

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:52.270
The PostgreSQL
interface for Spanner

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:54.250
is now available for preview.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:56.290
You can sign up for the
preview by filling out

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:58.630
the form in the
description below.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:00.310
Go ahead and try it
out and let us know

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:02.500
what you think in the comments.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

